#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204875} :activity/id #uuid "3ca1fe5c-e3e2-5af9-bb9f-82c8c2f00317" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050406 :topic/id #uuid "467c6e31-0c6e-5017-92e2-f1c8d38e5a4c" :topic/math-academy-id 5204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205125 :tutorial/content "Given a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, we know that its variance, which we may denote as $\\sigma_x^2$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_x^2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline x$ is the mean of $x$.\n\nThe summation term\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nis known as a **sum of squares** and is denoted $S_{xx}$. As we'll see, this quantity often appears in statistical analysis, so giving it a separate notation is useful.\n\nSimilarly, if we have a data set $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n$, then we define\n\n$$\nS_{yy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\overline y)^2\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we have the bivariate data set\n$(x_1, y_1), \\qquad (x_2, y_2),\\qquad \\ldots, \\qquad (x_n, y_n)$.\nThen, the covariance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)(y_i - \\overline y)\n$$\n\nand we may define $S_{xy}$ as\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nFinally, we can define the variance and covariance in terms of $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x}^{2} &= \\frac{S_{xx}}{n}, σ_{y}^{2} = \\frac{S_{yy}}{n}, Cov(x, y) = \\frac{S_{xy}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7e454c5-fb32-5adc-b4bd-0eb93ccd4ce7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10575 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "780a886e-f7ec-5134-8c19-6ef13b7e6958" :step/math-academy-id 226927 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203803 :question/id #uuid "6f0e3d4b-943b-5710-a996-ac4d3c68778d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16399"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0afa4d8-6d02-52c6-9836-12089de8f406" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204444}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums of Squares Given a Dataset or Summary Statistics"} :step/id #uuid "c4508f2d-5b01-5156-a636-efc40f9518ac" :step/math-academy-id 226928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204877}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204009 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203805 :question/id #uuid "7daa2313-4672-5624-aac2-e3b87b6ec366" :question/math-academy-id "e-16401"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "714000f9-caea-57a3-a8cb-6b8ec99960d1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204448}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Variance and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "b96fcd87-a442-5e10-907a-17ae7bf45eef" :step/math-academy-id 226931 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205126 :tutorial/content "Calculating $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ using their definitions can be cumbersome. Thankfully, we have the following shortcut formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\nS_{yy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i})^{2} \\\\\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}y_{i} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})(∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "c497f58a-e56b-5fde-9a53-c8553e8dcbcf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10576 :tutorial/title "Shortcut Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "d1fb1eb8-ff7c-5cd3-b9e5-580d6a2360ac" :step/math-academy-id 226934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204010 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203806 :question/id #uuid "9804df95-f494-5d99-acbd-97c69344d401" :question/math-academy-id "e-16402"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f0ed04b-f011-5818-952d-425a19e0cd5c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums of Squares Using the Shortcut Formula"} :step/id #uuid "88b4bb82-3260-512d-b14c-8e8a43f1aaa4" :step/math-academy-id 226935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204011 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203804 :question/id #uuid "489e5ffd-9e78-567b-b009-25d0c004825a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16400"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83df73fc-c0cc-5605-9b39-453de0c37cc7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233680}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating S(x,y) Using the Shortcut Formula"} :step/id #uuid "9f0e96ac-7d59-5c73-8d5d-f336df384031" :step/math-academy-id 226938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205148 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = ∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S_{xx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i}^{2} - 2x_{i} \\cdot x^(―) + x^(―)^{2})\n$$\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)2x_{i} \\cdot x^(―) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, since $2, \\overline x$, and $\\overline{x}^2$ are constants, we can write this as\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2x^(―) \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + x^(―)^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = n\\overline x, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = n\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2x^(―) \\cdot nx^(―) + x^(―)^{2} \\cdot n \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2nx^(―)^{2} + nx^(―)^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - nx^(―)^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for $S_{yy}$ and $S_{xy}$ are similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "7dab2448-da48-5100-9373-89c074ae5326" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10852 :tutorial/title "Proving the Shortcut Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "ddc81863-5abf-5730-bfbc-1afae5ed8857" :step/math-academy-id 226941}] :activity/title "Sums of Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204149}]}
